msgid "<p>Below is a list of conditions your child/ adult may currently experience or have experienced in the past. When you check that your child/adult has issues in a category, you will be asked to provide additional information.</p>"
"\n<table class=\"table\">"
"\n  <tr>"
"\n    <th>Category</th>"
"\n    <th>Examples</th>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n  <tr>"
"\n    <td>Growth/ feeding</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced significant weight or growth-related concerns. This may include failure to thrive or difficulty gaining weight, or issues with excessive weight gain and/or hyperphagia (unusual hunger or difficulty feeling full). </td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n  <tr>"
"\n    <td>Eyes/ vision</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced vision or eye-related concerns. This may include a diagnosis of cortical vision impairment (CVI), strabismus (misalignment of the eyes), or other visual processing difficulties. They may have seen an ophthalmologist, optometrist, or neurologist to assess and manage these issues."
"\n</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n <tr>"
"\n    <td>Brain/ nervous system (neurological issues)</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced neurological or brain-related concerns. This may include seizures or epilepsy. It may also include cortical myoclonus (also known as non-epileptic myoclonus or tremors), which can appear as sudden jerks or twitching movements not caused by seizures.</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n <tr>"
"\n    <td>Behaviour/ psychiatric</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has been diagnosed with autism spectrum disorders, schizophrenia, anxiety, aggression, repetitive behaviours, and depression. Your child/ adult may have seen a developmental paediatrician, psychologist, psychiatrist, or therapist for these issues.</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n <tr>"
"\n   <td>Muscles/ skeletal</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced muscular or skeletal concerns. This may include issues such as tight heel cords, toe walking, or scoliosis. These concerns may affect mobility, balance, or posture, and your child/adult may have seen a neurologist, orthopaedist, or another specialist to assess and manage these issues.</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n <tr>"
"\n   <td>Lungs/ breathing</td>"
"\n    <td> Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced lung or breathing-related concerns. This may include a history of aspiration (food, liquid, or saliva entering the airway) or recurrent pneumonia, which can result from difficulties with swallowing or airway protection. They may have required review by a respiratory specialist, pulmonologist, speech pathologist, or ENT (ear, nose, and throat doctor) to investigate and manage these concerns, especially if hospitalisation or feeding support was needed.</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n <tr>"
"\n   <td>Digestive system</td>"
"\n    <td>Your child or adult with Angelman syndrome has experienced issues with the stomach, intestines, or overall digestion, such as severe constipation, gastroesophageal reflux (reflux), vomiting (including cyclic), or other difficulties with how food moves through or is processed by the GI (gastrointestinal) tract. Your child/adult may have been seen by a gastroenterologist (GI doctor) or other specialist to assess and manage these issues.</td>"
"\n  </tr>"
"\n</table><br>"
